              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Mandarin for laundry shop, then the Mandarin for to dry-clean."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: zāogāo",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "zāogāo — \"oh no; what a mess\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "zāogāo wǒ de qiánbāo diū le — Oh no, I've lost my wallet!"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Its characters come later; for now it is a word for the ear."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "zāogāo is built from zāo 'dregs' and gāo 'cake', both inherited from Middle Chinese (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription): tsaw, glossed 'dregs', and probably the kaw that the dictionary writes with another form of the character, glossed with a word for gruel. How the pair came to mean a mess is not settled."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for saying no and asking."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
